1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,720
Welcome to the evocative hall of Candelabras, so named because the Spilio Thames found in

2
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:11,160
this section of the route, resemble, in shape and color.

3
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:18,560
Elegan 19th century Candelabras, with wax seemingly dripping slowly from the candles.

4
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:23,520
We are now in the first of the four halls we will visit along a route of approximately

5
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:25,120
one kilometer.

6
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:31,480
In reality, however, the Grotta del Boe-Marino is part of a much larger car's system.

7
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:37,440
The cave you are visiting today connects through underground galleries to three other cavities,

8
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:43,360
the Grotta de Supalu, the Grotta de Sumo-Lente, and the Grotta de Montelongos.

9
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:49,080
There are therefore four external entrances leading to a single vast underground system,

10
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:53,280
with approximately 75 kilometers explored so far.

11
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:55,560
It is important to stress this.

12
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:58,120
The system is still being explored.

13
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:05,000
We do not yet know its true extent, with its 75 kilometers already documented, it is currently

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,200
the second longest car's system in Italy.

15
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:14,840
The Grotta del Boe-Marino is the only part of the system accessible to the public and is

16
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:17,600
divided into three branches.

17
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:24,200
The Northern Branch, approximately 9 kilometers long, currently closed to the public.

18
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:29,680
The central branch, 5 kilometers long, accessible only to cave divers.

19
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:32,760
The Southern Branch, 8 kilometers long.

20
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:38,120
The one we are visiting today, of which only the first kilometer has been open to visitors

21
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:42,440
since 1983, the year of its first public opening.

22
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:45,000
The Southern Branch is still active.

23
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,120
Along the route you will notice dripping water, a sign that the formations are still slowly

24
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:51,600
developing.

25
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:57,120
The cave is constantly changing, even if imperceptibly, to our eyes.

26
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:02,840
But the cave also changes through another far more powerful force, the erosive action

27
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:09,320
of an underground river that still flows today and continues to carve out these galleries.

28
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:13,960
For almost the entire length of our route, there is sea water beneath our feet.

29
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:18,360
At the end of the route, we will instead encounter an underground river.

30
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:22,840
A head of us will flow fresh, clear and silent water.

31
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:27,720
It is precisely this water course that is the principle architect of the entire car's

32
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:29,000
system.

33
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,200
Through its erosive force exerted over millions of years, it has carved through the limestone,

34
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:41,000
shaping galleries, widening cavities, and contributing to the formation of most of the

35
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,960
75 kilometers explored to date.

36
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:50,800
As you walk through these halls, try to imagine a time when the spaces you see were completely

37
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:53,120
submerged.

38
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:58,640
During the early stages of its formation, the cave was filled with fresh water right up to

39
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,360
the ceiling.

40
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:06,400
The mighty underground river occupied every space, exerting a continuous erosive action

41
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:13,120
on the rock in its slow and ceaseless journey in search of a natural outlet.

42
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:16,200
That outlet was found at the sea.

43
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:21,600
The current entrance to the Grata del Boe marino coincides in fact with the mouth of that

44
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:27,840
underground river, the terminal stretch of a vast system shaped by water over geological

45
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:29,320
time.

46
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:34,480
When the river opened its outlet to the sea, the water level inside the cave gradually

47
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:35,800
dropped.

48
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:40,520
The halls emptied and the sea became part of the underground environment.

49
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:47,040
Only then dropped by drop in silence and darkness did all the formations you see today

50
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:50,080
begin to slowly grow and take shape.

51
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:55,440
What we are observing is not simply a cave, but the result of a long and uninterrupted

52
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:58,280
dialogue between water and stone.

53
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:03,720
Natural work of art shaped by the silent force of a river hidden in the heart of the mountain.

